<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Augustine of Hippo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">354–430</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> · Also known as Aurelius Augustinus, Saint Augustine</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Augustine of Hippo was a Christian theologian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> from Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thagaste">Thagaste</a> in Numidia. After teaching rhetoric in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, he converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in 386 and became bishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippo-regius">Hippo Regius</a> in 395, where he wrote against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donatism">Donatists</a> and Pelagians. His Confessions and City of God made him the most influential of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-christendom">Latin Church</a> Fathers, and his remains rest at Pavia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo.jpg" alt="Augustine of Hippo" width="320" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Philippe de Champaigne · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Augustine_by_Philippe_de_Champaigne.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A disputed episcopal election at Carthage around 311 started the Donatist schism, which divided African Christians for a century and prompted <b>Augustine of Hippo</b>, who had studied and taught at Carthage, to develop his ideas on the church and the use of coercion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Inside, <b>Saint Augustine</b> and his priests prayed for relief; on 28 August 430, three months into the siege, the 75-year-old bishop died.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vandal-kingdom">Vandal Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The writers of the early church, the Church Fathers, shaped its doctrine: Irenaeus, Origen and Tertullian in the 2nd and 3rd centuries; Athanasius, the Cappadocian Fathers, John Chrysostom, Ambrose, Jerome and <b>Augustine of Hippo</b> in the 4th and 5th.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>For centuries Manichaeism was known mainly through the attacks of its enemies, above all <b>Augustine of Hippo</b>, who had been a Hearer for about nine years before becoming a Christian and wrote many works against it.</blockquote><a href="/religion/manichaeism">Manichaeism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Christian writers such as Tertullian, Ambrose and <b>Augustine of Hippo</b>, whose Confessions and City of God shaped Western thought, made Latin the language of theology.</blockquote><a href="/language/latin">Latin · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Punic lasted far longer, arguably into the time of <b>Augustine of Hippo</b>, and until the 6th century CE, perhaps even the 9th.</blockquote><a href="/language/phoenician">Phoenician · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thagaste">Thagaste</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippo-regius">Hippo Regius</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.27,7.93&amp;year=403" aria-label="Thagaste on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4750 855 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Augustine of Hippo"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5200 1125h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5200 1125h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.27,7.93&amp;year=403">What was here in 403 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Thou awakest us to delight in Thy praise; for Thou madest us for Thyself, and our heart is restless, until it repose in Thee.&#8221;</p><footer>Confessions, book 1 &#183; tr. E. B. Pusey, 1838 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3296/pg3296-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Give me chastity and continency, only not yet.&#8221;</p><footer>Confessions, book 8 &#183; tr. E. B. Pusey, 1838 &#183; the prayer of his youth, as he recalls it &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3296/pg3296-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Justice being taken away, then, what are kingdoms but great robberies? For what are robberies themselves, but little kingdoms?&#8221;</p><footer>The City of God, book 4, chapter 4 &#183; tr. George Wilson, in Marcus Dods's edition, 1871 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/45304/pg45304-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Too late loved I Thee, O Thou Beauty of ancient days, yet ever new! too late I loved Thee! And behold, Thou wert within, and I abroad, and there I searched for Thee…&#8221;</p><footer>Confessions, book 10 &#183; tr. E. B. Pusey, 1838 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3296/pg3296-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;What then is time? If no one asks me, I know: if I wish to explain it to one that asketh, I know not…&#8221;</p><footer>Confessions, book 11 &#183; tr. E. B. Pusey, 1838 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3296/pg3296-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
